Specific comments on what is readable and what is not:
What I noticed was readable:
  BlockCollision has clear method names that describe the exact collision operation being performed. Methods like IsOnGround, TryJump, MoveHorizontally, MoveVertically, HasGroundAhead, and UpdateVerticalMovement make these main collision operations easy to identify.
The BlockCollisionState class keeps information about blocks, foot inset, and ground state together. This makes it much easier to understand which information the collision system needs to remember for every game object.
The revised Physics.cs is especially straightforward to read. Gravity and vertical velocity are stored in one place, while Jump, Launch, SetGravity, and Apply provide their basic physics operations.
PlayerCollision is easy to follow because HandleContact uses switch to separate doors, player triggers, and enemies. HandleEnemyContact separates enemy-specific damage behavior instead of putting everything in one method.

What I noticed could be improved:
  BlockCollision contains several different kinds of collision behavior including player movement, moving platforms, enemy ledge detection, and projectile impacts. The individual methods are readable, but the class contains so many different behaviors that it can take time to determine where new collision features are to be added.
UpdatePlayerMovement is also fairly long and performs many different operations like moving platforms, horizontal movement, gravity, and ground-state changes. Potentially splitting some of these operations into helper methods could make this sequence easier to follow without having to change the collision behavior.


