Specific comments on code quality:
What I noticed:
  BlockCollision provides a reusable collision system for different game objects instead of requiring the Player and Enemy to perform their own block checks. It allows for the same BlockCollision instance to manage different objects while keeping their supporting block and ground state separate.
The separation between Physics and BlockCollision is useful. Physics handles gravity and vertical velocity, while BlockCollision resolves that movement against the level geometry. For example, UpdateVerticalMovement first applies Physics and only then uses BlockCollisionHelper to resolve the resulting position against blocks.
PlayerCollision also keeps player contact behaviour separate from block movement. It handles doors, triggers, and enemy contacts without adding those cases directly to the player's movement code.
An issue I noticed during testing is an edge case when the player is attacked while moving upward on a moving platform. This seems to be a collision error, and this interaction certainly needs to be looked into so that platform movement and knockback have a consistent order.

Hypothetical change:
  If ladders are added in the next sprint, our collision system will need to support another type of player movement that is different from normal gravity-based movement. I would recommend adding ladder detection separately from the existing block collision checks and giving the player a clearer climbing state.
While climbing, the player's vertical movement should be controlled by the ladder rather than normal gravity. The player would return to normal physics when leaving the ladder. This should also be tested with the existing moving platform collision error to make sure climbing does not face similar conflicts.
